{#
  Helm values for Harbor, deployed as a proxy cache for the registries
  the cluster pulls from.

  Scoped to caching. Trivy is disabled, and nothing here pushes homelab-built
  images — the OTBR image still reaches the cluster through
  `docker save | ctr -n k8s.io images import`, which needs no registry.
  Enabling scanning later is this file plus a PVC for the vulnerability
  database.

  expose.type is clusterIP rather than ingress so the chart deploys its own
  nginx component, which does Harbor's internal path routing (/v2 to the
  registry, /service to the token issuer, /api and / to core and portal)
  behind a single Service. cluster/registry.yml's HTTPRoutes then all point at
  that one Service and differ only in which of them carries the Authentik
  ForwardAuth filter. Using the chart's own ingress instead would put that
  path routing in a resource the chart owns, where the filter cannot be
  applied selectively.

  expose.tls.enabled is false because Traefik terminates TLS at the gateway
  using the wildcard certificate that already covers *.{{ dns_zone }}.
  externalURL is still https: Harbor builds the Www-Authenticate realm that
  containerd follows to fetch a token out of this value, so a http:// here
  sends every client to a scheme the gateway redirects away from.

  harborAdminPassword is supplied through existingSecretAdminPassword rather
  than inline, so the credential stays out of the rendered Helm release blob.
  Setting it at all matters beyond hygiene: Harbor 2.15.0 and earlier accepted
  the built-in Harbor12345 for the admin account regardless of configuration
  (CVE-2026-4404, fixed in 2.15.1). This deployment is on a fixed version and
  does not rely on that.

  updateStrategy is Recreate. The registry's blobs live on a ReadWriteOnce
  local PersistentVolume, so a RollingUpdate would hold the volume in the old
  pod while the new one waits to attach it, and the upgrade would stall until
  the deadline. Recreate stops the old pod first, at the cost of the cache
  being briefly unavailable during an upgrade — which degrades to upstream
  pulls rather than to failures.

  The chart's default containerSecurityContext already drops all capabilities,
  disallows privilege escalation, sets seccompProfile RuntimeDefault, and runs
  as non-root, so Harbor satisfies the cluster's fail-closed `restricted` Pod
  Security Admission default without a namespace opt-down and without
  restating any of it here.

  Harbor's images bake in UID/GID 10000, so the pods are not remapped to the
  shared nonroot_uid the way the charts that accept a runAsUser are. The blob
  directory on the host is chowned to match, in cluster/registry.yml.

  PostgreSQL is a CloudNativePG Cluster managed outside this chart, so the
  bundled database is disabled and the password is read from the same
  cnpg-formatted Secret CloudNativePG provisions the user from. Valkey (the
  chart's internal cache, named redis in the values regardless) stays bundled
  — it holds job state and API cache, both regenerable, and running it through
  CloudNativePG's operator is not an option since it is not PostgreSQL.

  Variables:
    harbor_hostname:         FQDN Harbor is reached at, derived from dns_zone.
    harbor_storage_class:    StorageClass for the volumes that are not the blob
                             store (default: longhorn).
    harbor_db_name:          PostgreSQL database name for Harbor's core.
    harbor_db_username:      PostgreSQL username for Harbor.
    harbor_ca_bundle_secret: Name of the Secret holding the homelab CA under a
                             ca.crt key, created by the playbook.
    harbor_namespace:        Namespace Harbor is deployed into, used to address
                             the CloudNativePG read-write Service.
    dns_zone:                DNS zone served by Bind9. Read from group_vars.
#}
expose:
  type: clusterIP
  tls:
    enabled: false

externalURL: 'https://{{ harbor_hostname }}'

existingSecretAdminPassword: harbor-credentials
existingSecretAdminPasswordKey: admin-password
existingSecretSecretKey: harbor-credentials

updateStrategy:
  type: Recreate

# Injects the homelab CA into the trust store of core, jobservice, registry,
# and trivy. Harbor's core needs it to verify Authentik's certificate when it
# fetches the OIDC discovery document, which is what lets oidc_verify_cert stay
# on. The Secret's key must be named ca.crt.
caBundleSecretName: '{{ harbor_ca_bundle_secret }}'

persistence:
  enabled: true
  resourcePolicy: keep
  persistentVolumeClaim:
    # Claimed rather than provisioned: the blob store is a static local volume
    # on the node flagged registry_cache_node, created by
    # registry-blob-storage.yml.j2.
    registry:
      existingClaim: harbor-registry-blobs
    jobservice:
      jobLog:
        storageClass: '{{ harbor_storage_class }}'
        size: 1Gi
    redis:
      storageClass: '{{ harbor_storage_class }}'
      size: 1Gi

database:
  type: external
  external:
    host: harbor-db-rw.{{ harbor_namespace }}.svc
    port: '5432'
    username: '{{ harbor_db_username }}'
    coreDatabase: '{{ harbor_db_name }}'
    # Key must be named `password`, which is what the cnpg-formatted Secret
    # already uses.
    existingSecret: harbor-db-credentials
    sslmode: disable

redis:
  type: internal

trivy:
  enabled: false
